1
00:00:00,410 --> 00:00:03,003
In 1969, Apollo 11 landed on the

2
00:00:03,023 --> 00:00:05,697
Moon. Part of its lander is still

3
00:00:05,717 --> 00:00:06,942
up there today.

4
00:00:07,562 --> 00:00:09,456
We're a team from the Philippines,

5
00:00:09,476 --> 00:00:11,278
and this is Space Archaeologist.

6
00:00:15,498 --> 00:00:17,574
Our challenge is called Abandoned but not

7
00:00:17,594 --> 00:00:18,105
Forgotten.

8
00:00:18,675 --> 00:00:21,655
Since the 1960s, NASA has left machines

9
00:00:21,675 --> 00:00:23,521
on the Moon and on Mars.

10
00:00:24,041 --> 00:00:27,029
A laser reflector that scientists still bounce

11
00:00:27,049 --> 00:00:29,318
light off today. A rover that drove

12
00:00:29,338 --> 00:00:31,235
more than a marathon on Mars.

13
00:00:31,755 --> 00:00:34,155
But the challenge notes that kids mostly

14
00:00:34,175 --> 00:00:35,849
hear about today's missions.

15
00:00:35,869 --> 00:00:38,209
The old machines live in dense websites

16
00:00:38,229 --> 00:00:39,076
and textbooks.

17
00:00:39,596 --> 00:00:41,757
We believe kids learn best by doing.

18
00:00:41,777 --> 00:00:43,636
So we asked: what if kids could

19
00:00:43,656 --> 00:00:45,595
dig these stories up themselves?

20
00:00:49,465 --> 00:00:52,471
Space Archaeologist is a free browser game.

21
00:00:52,491 --> 00:00:54,512
Kids aged 7 to 12 explore the

22
00:00:54,532 --> 00:00:56,905
real machines left on the Moon and

23
00:00:56,925 --> 00:00:59,389
Mars, and learn their true stories.

24
00:00:59,909 --> 00:01:01,328
You design an explorer,

25
00:01:01,348 --> 00:01:03,517
board the ship, and pick a mission.

26
00:01:03,537 --> 00:01:05,082
Then come real NASA film,

27
00:01:05,102 --> 00:01:06,646
a launch, digs and games,

28
00:01:06,666 --> 00:01:08,105
and a badge at the end.

29
00:01:08,625 --> 00:01:10,506
And Nova, a friendly AI guide,

30
00:01:10,526 --> 00:01:12,554
answers questions the whole way.

31
00:01:16,524 --> 00:01:17,604
Here's the real game,

32
00:01:17,624 --> 00:01:19,301
recorded from the current build.

33
00:01:19,741 --> 00:01:21,921
Press start. Nova gives a short tour.

34
00:01:21,941 --> 00:01:24,439
Type a nickname, and design your explorer.

35
00:01:25,909 --> 00:01:26,736
This is the ship.

36
00:01:26,756 --> 00:01:28,302
Two missions are ready to play.

37
00:01:28,772 --> 00:01:31,411
Mission one opens with real Apollo 11

38
00:01:31,431 --> 00:01:32,509
film from NASA.

39
00:01:32,979 --> 00:01:34,280
Then you pull the lever.

40
00:01:34,300 --> 00:01:35,546
The Saturn V lifts off,

41
00:01:35,566 --> 00:01:36,887
and you fly to the Moon.

42
00:01:37,357 --> 00:01:38,951
On the Moon, the path at the

43
00:01:38,971 --> 00:01:40,162
top shows every step.

44
00:01:40,182 --> 00:01:42,545
Walk around, scan, and follow the glints.

45
00:01:43,015 --> 00:01:44,715
Every find starts with a dig.

46
00:01:44,735 --> 00:01:45,921
Brush the dust away.

47
00:01:46,391 --> 00:01:47,492
Then turn it around,

48
00:01:47,512 --> 00:01:49,342
and open its glowing parts to see

49
00:01:49,362 --> 00:01:50,427
what each one does.

50
00:01:51,847 --> 00:01:53,553
This is the laser reflector.

51
00:01:53,573 --> 00:01:54,662
Send a laser ping,

52
00:01:54,682 --> 00:01:56,512
and watch the light come back.

53
00:01:56,532 --> 00:01:58,443
Scientists still do this today.

54
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:01,506
Then comes a quick quiz.

55
00:02:01,526 --> 00:02:02,685
Hold up one, two,

56
00:02:02,705 --> 00:02:05,041
three or four fingers to answer A,

57
00:02:05,061 --> 00:02:05,755
B, C or D.

58
00:02:07,175 --> 00:02:08,295
In the Rock Box,

59
00:02:08,315 --> 00:02:10,719
you catch Moon rocks with your own

60
00:02:10,739 --> 00:02:13,571
hands. Apollo 11 brought home about 21.5

61
00:02:13,591 --> 00:02:14,304
kilograms.

62
00:02:15,724 --> 00:02:16,989
Next, rebuild the lander,

63
00:02:17,009 --> 00:02:18,757
and put its journey back in order.

64
00:02:20,177 --> 00:02:22,419
The mission report is a quiz nobody

65
00:02:22,439 --> 00:02:24,745
fails. A wrong answer just becomes a

66
00:02:24,765 --> 00:02:25,088
hint.

67
00:02:26,508 --> 00:02:27,522
Then you earn a badge,

68
00:02:27,542 --> 00:02:28,859
and the chapter is complete.

69
00:02:29,329 --> 00:02:31,885
On Mars, you keep the rover Opportunity

70
00:02:31,905 --> 00:02:34,131
going. Wipe the dust off its solar

71
00:02:34,151 --> 00:02:36,707
panels, just like the real Martian wind

72
00:02:36,727 --> 00:02:36,991
did.

73
00:02:37,461 --> 00:02:39,453
And any time, kids can simply ask

74
00:02:39,473 --> 00:02:40,388
Nova, out loud.

75
00:02:42,288 --> 00:02:43,920
Player: Nova, how far away is the

76
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:44,191
Moon?

77
00:02:44,911 --> 00:02:47,070
The game turns the question into text

78
00:02:47,090 --> 00:02:48,425
right on this computer.

79
00:02:48,445 --> 00:02:50,035
Then Nova checks her facts.

80
00:02:51,888 --> 00:02:52,978
Nova: Ooh, great question!

81
00:02:56,988 --> 00:03:00,307
The Moon is about 385,000 kilometres away

82
00:03:00,327 --> 00:03:00,978
from us.

83
00:03:01,348 --> 00:03:02,958
Did you know it even drifts a

84
00:03:02,978 --> 00:03:05,338
little bit further away every single year?

85
00:03:06,508 --> 00:03:08,283
Every feature is there for a reason.

86
00:03:10,008 --> 00:03:11,995
Kids ask lots of questions.

87
00:03:12,015 --> 00:03:14,224
Nova answers by voice or text,

88
00:03:14,244 --> 00:03:15,859
stays on space topics,

89
00:03:15,879 --> 00:03:18,332
and uses facts with real sources.

90
00:03:18,802 --> 00:03:20,746
Moving your real hands to scan,

91
00:03:20,766 --> 00:03:23,027
grab and ping makes learning active.

92
00:03:23,047 --> 00:03:24,611
And it's optional. Mouse,

93
00:03:24,631 --> 00:03:26,849
keyboard and touch work everywhere.

94
00:03:27,319 --> 00:03:29,092
And every story is read aloud,

95
00:03:29,112 --> 00:03:31,483
with captions, so younger kids and early

96
00:03:31,503 --> 00:03:32,997
readers can follow along.

97
00:03:33,467 --> 00:03:35,595
It's all built on NASA data.

98
00:03:35,615 --> 00:03:38,050
275 facts, each linked to one of

99
00:03:38,070 --> 00:03:40,735
46 NASA sources, and checked by our

100
00:03:40,755 --> 00:03:41,599
fact tests.

101
00:03:42,069 --> 00:03:44,296
Nova can even check live NASA feeds:

102
00:03:44,316 --> 00:03:46,169
asteroids passing Earth today,

103
00:03:46,189 --> 00:03:48,623
space weather, and the weather on Mars.

104
00:03:49,093 --> 00:03:50,827
It's built for kids' safety.

105
00:03:50,847 --> 00:03:53,520
No accounts. Camera and voice are processed

106
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:56,089
on the computer. And grown-ups can switch

107
00:03:56,109 --> 00:03:56,798
the AI off.

108
00:03:57,268 --> 00:03:58,749
Captions are always on.

109
00:03:58,769 --> 00:04:01,293
There's calm motion, an easy-read font,

110
00:04:01,313 --> 00:04:02,664
and no camera needed.

111
00:04:02,684 --> 00:04:05,404
Even offline, Nova answers from the game's

112
00:04:05,424 --> 00:04:06,403
own fact sheet.

113
00:04:10,273 --> 00:04:12,163
Space Archaeologist is free,

114
00:04:12,183 --> 00:04:14,141
and it runs in a web browser.

115
00:04:14,161 --> 00:04:16,207
No downloads, and no accounts.

116
00:04:16,677 --> 00:04:19,063
It's designed so a 7-year-old can finish

117
00:04:19,083 --> 00:04:21,228
it, and older kids still chase three

118
00:04:21,248 --> 00:04:21,609
stars.

119
00:04:22,079 --> 00:04:24,286
Our goal: every kid walks away knowing

120
00:04:24,306 --> 00:04:25,517
what was left behind,

121
00:04:25,537 --> 00:04:27,861
why it stayed, and that scientists still

122
00:04:27,881 --> 00:04:28,291
use it.

123
00:04:28,761 --> 00:04:30,822
Three more missions are planned:

124
00:04:30,842 --> 00:04:32,187
Viking 1, Pioneer 10,

125
00:04:32,207 --> 00:04:33,117
and Voyager 1.

126
00:04:33,587 --> 00:04:36,728
Space Archaeologist turns the machines NASA left

127
00:04:36,748 --> 00:04:38,901
behind into a game kids can play,

128
00:04:38,921 --> 00:04:41,357
so their stories are never forgotten.

129
00:04:42,577 --> 00:04:43,647
Play it free, right now,

130
00:04:43,667 --> 00:04:44,394
in your browser.
